WEBVTT

00:00:04.975 --> 00:00:08.430 line:85%,start
Josephus was widely read throughout
the medieval Latin West.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:12.180 line:91%,start
Latin Josephus 3.0 lets us compare

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:13.770 line:91%,start
his <i>Jewish War</i> in Greek,

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:16.760 line:91%,start
in its late-antique Latin translation,

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:21.130 line:85%,start
and in Thomas Lodge’s English
translation of 1602.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:26.320 line:85%,start
As we advance to the next passage,
all three texts move together.

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:30.295 line:85%,start
Even Lodge’s marginal notes
can be displayed.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:35.720 line:85%,start
But Josephus was not simply translated.
He was also rewritten.

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:39.550 line:85%,start
The <i>De excidio Hierosolymitano</i>
is a late-antique

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:42.470 line:85%,start
Christian adaptation
of the <i>Jewish War</i>.

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:45.670 line:85%,start
Here we can open the corresponding
passage in Josephus

00:00:45.745 --> 00:00:48.820 line:85%,start
and compare how the two accounts
present the Roman arguments

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:51.520 line:85%,start
for destroying
the Temple of Jerusalem.

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:57.970 line:85%,start
We can also recover traces
of medieval reading.

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:01.170 line:91%,start
In the <i>Jewish Antiquities</i>,

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:04.010 line:85%,start
passages containing annotations
can be highlighted,

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.170 line:91%,start
and their transcriptions examined.

00:01:06.835 --> 00:01:11.500 line:85%,start
This example includes a marginal
annotation from Codex Bodmer 98.

00:01:13.645 --> 00:01:17.510 line:85%,start
Finally, we can search across
different works and translations.

00:01:18.055 --> 00:01:21.665 line:85%,start
Searching for the exact phrase
“holy of holies”

00:01:22.125 --> 00:01:25.430 line:85%,start
produces five results
across three works.

00:01:26.105 --> 00:01:29.550 line:85%,start
Notice that the same passage
of the <i>Jewish War</i> appears twice,

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:32.860 line:91%,start
once in Whiston’s English translation,

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:34.710 line:91%,start
and once in Lodge’s.

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:39.850 line:85%,start
And these are only a few
of the possibilities.

00:01:40.295 --> 00:01:45.550 line:85%,start
Explore the Latin Josephus Project
at latinjosephus.org.
